1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:12,000
folder B dammit— how MANY times do I have to tell you man?! It's 34 employees!

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Have you, or anyone you know, rage-quit a coding session? Join the club, buddy.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Our agents have a memory problem. Two weeks later, it's like you don't remember what I said.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
So this is how my girlfriend feels.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Every agent wakes up stateless. All that intelligence—and no continuity.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
But people tell me the fix is simple…

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Just get a 6-million-billion context window, bro? That isn't memory. It's a TAX.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
That's big-lab work. I mean, I would, but...

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
My Meta money hasn't landed yet. So until then? I'll just put the fries in the bag. What CAN I do?

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
First: my girlfriend mentioned her friend's dog once. Weeks later, still there. Why does THAT stick?

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Second: how does a memory show up exactly when I need it, when I never searched for it?

12
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Third: how is it all wired together—so one memory drags the next one up with it?

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Fifty-four papers later: predictive coding gates surprise (the Observer).

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Tiered storage links synapses (the Scribe). Spreading activation recalls (the Retriever).

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Simulate the brain's solutions. Never simulate its constraints.

16
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
All theory. How do you WATCH a memory form? Build a brain you can look at.

17
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Tell it a rule once. Watch the right side file it: checks if new, links to the graph.

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Truth changes: 'I'm vegetarian.' No duplicates. It supersedes non-destructively.

19
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:05,000
New session, zero context. Give it a command: activation spreads, pulling rules.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Take the memory away...

21
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,000
...and it's a goldfish: 'I don't know.' Flip it ON: the genius is back.

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Then it sleeps: replays the day's chaos and compresses moments into lessons.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
How do you test this? I built an automated harness: a second agent to interrogate the first.

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
When an agent gets it wrong, you don't guess: open the graph and trace the exact path.

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Built on Meta: powered by Muse Spark via Meta Model API, with local Llama fallback.

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
IDEs that know the stack. Personal assistants. Robot brains that learn.

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And yes... everything I was mad at my girlfriend for... and her birthday.

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Stop treating AI like an amnesiac. Give it a mind that persists. Tottie. Built on Meta.
